 [Demo Video](https://youtu.be/-bf9_vbjbSQ)
 
+## Web UI (recommended)
+
+There's now a browser-based control panel that wraps the CLI: a setup step for your API key, a prompt editor for every instruction sent to the model, and a "Generate" tab with a live log stream.
+
+```bash
+cd server # If in root directory
+deno task webui
+```
+
+Then open http://localhost:8787. From there:
+- **Setup** — save your `OPENAI_API_KEY` (writes to `server/.env`).
+- **Prompts** — every system prompt in the generation pipeline, grouped by stage, with a text editor and "Reset to default." Edits are saved to `server/webui/prompts/prompt-overrides.json` and take effect on your very next run — no restart needed.
+- **Generate** — describe your world, pick a chapter count, and watch each generation step stream in as it happens. Check "skip ROM build" to only generate the story/character JSON without needing Wine or a base ROM yet.
+
+The ROM-building step (turning the generated data into an actual `.gba` file) still needs the same external tools as before — see "Setup" below and the "Common Commands" section — the Web UI just shells out to the same `romBuilder/run.sh` used by the CLI.
+
+## Using a local model with Ollama
+
+The generation pipeline can run entirely against a local [Ollama](https://ollama.com) server instead of OpenAI. Set this up from the Web UI's **Setup** tab (pick "Ollama (local)"), or by hand in `server/.env`:
+
+```
+AI_PROVIDER=ollama
+OLLAMA_MODEL=qwen3-fe   # the enlarged-context model you create below
+OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1   # optional, this is the default
+```
+
+### If Ollama is on Windows and this runs in WSL
+
+This is the usual setup, and it's the right one — **don't reinstall Ollama inside WSL.** Windows-native Ollama already has GPU acceleration and your downloaded models; a second WSL copy would re-download everything and need CUDA passthrough configured.
+
+Run `ollama` commands (`pull`, `create`, `list`) in **PowerShell on Windows**, not in WSL. Then connect WSL to it:
+
+1. **Make Ollama listen beyond localhost.** By default it binds to `127.0.0.1`, which WSL2 can't reach because it's a separate network namespace. On Windows, set the environment variable `OLLAMA_HOST=0.0.0.0` and restart Ollama. (System Properties → Environment Variables, or `setx OLLAMA_HOST "0.0.0.0"` then restart the Ollama app.)
+2. **Allow port 11434 through Windows Firewall** — permitting it on private networks is enough.
+3. **Point WSL at the Windows host.** If `OLLAMA_BASE_URL` isn't set, this project tries to auto-detect the Windows host from WSL's default route, so it often just works. If it doesn't, run `ipconfig` on Windows, take the WSL/vEthernet adapter's IPv4 address, and set:
+
+```
+OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://<windows-ip>:11434/v1
+```
+
+A generation run checks this at startup and, if it can't connect, prints exactly which addresses it tried and what to do — including telling you the right `OLLAMA_BASE_URL` if it finds Ollama at an address other than the configured one.
+
+### Requires Ollama 0.5 or newer
+
+Generation relies on JSON-schema enforcement (Ollama constrains decoding so the model can only emit conforming JSON). That landed in Ollama 0.5. On older versions the schema is dropped and the model is merely *asked* to produce the right shape, which local models handle badly — in testing qwen3 responded by echoing the schema back as its answer. Check with `ollama --version`.
+
+### Give the model a big enough context window
+
+**This is the step people get wrong.** Ollama defaults to a 4,096-token context, and this pipeline routinely sends more than that — a single unit-placement prompt includes the map's terrain grid, which alone can exceed 4,000 tokens. Ollama does not error when a prompt is too long; it silently truncates it, and the model then produces nonsense that fails validation.
+
+Create a variant of your model with a larger context:
+
+```bash
+cat > Modelfile <<'EOF'
+FROM qwen3:14b
+PARAMETER num_ctx 32768
+EOF
+
+ollama create qwen3-fe -f Modelfile
+```
+
+Then set `OLLAMA_MODEL=qwen3-fe   # the enlarged-context model you create below`. Adjust `num_ctx` to whatever your VRAM allows — 32k is comfortable, 16k is usually workable, 8k is marginal.
+
+### What to expect
+
+- **Smaller models fail validation more often.** That's mostly fine: every generation call retries with corrective feedback, so failures usually self-correct. It does mean runs are slower and use more tokens than the retry counts suggest.
+- **Reasoning models need their thinking suppressed.** Qwen3 and similar emit a `<think>` block that isn't valid JSON. A `/no_think` directive is appended automatically for models whose names look like reasoners, so this should be handled for you.
+- **Two scripts still require OpenAI**, because they send images to the model: `map-processing/ai/define-map-areas.ts` and `ai/portraits/portrait-metadata-creation/generate-portrait-metadata.ts`. Both are one-off preprocessing steps and their results are already committed to this repo (`all-map-options.ts`, `all-portrait-options.ts`), so **normal game generation never calls them**. You only need OpenAI if you want to add new maps or portraits.
+
+## Adding your own maps
+
+The AI picks each chapter's battlefield from a pool of maps. To add to that pool, drop Tiled **`.tmx`** files into `server/map-processing/maps-to-import/` and run:
+
+```bash
+cd server
+deno task import-maps
+```
+
+Each map is parsed for its dimensions, terrain grid, and points of interest (chests, villages, thrones, stairs), split into named regions the AI can reason about, and added to the pool. Existing maps are left alone, so this is safe to re-run.
+
+### Format requirements
+
+**`.tmx` (Tiled) is the only supported format.** The ROM build runs maps through `tmx2ea`, which reads Tiled files exclusively. If you have `.map`, `.mar`, `.fmp` or similar, open them in [Tiled](https://www.mapeditor.org/) and save as `.tmx` first — those extensions aren't a format this toolchain can read, and Windows' descriptions of them ("linker address map", "Access report shortcut") are just generic guesses for extensions it doesn't recognize.
+
+Two things must also line up, both checked at import time:
+
+1. **The tileset inside the .tmx must be named with its 8 hex IDs**, e.g. `3C00CE3E` — that's ObjectType `3C`, PaletteID `CE`, TileConfig `3E`. This is how the ROM knows which graphics to load.
+2. **That tileset's `.png` must exist** in `romBuilder/Maps/Tilesets/`.
+
+Maps failing either check are skipped with an explanation rather than imported. That's deliberate: a map with a missing or misnamed tileset builds into a chapter with corrupted graphics, which is much harder to diagnose later than a message at import time.
+
+### Area detection
+
+Areas are derived directly from the tile data — no AI call, so this works on a local Ollama setup and costs nothing. You get names like "Northwest Throne Room" or "Southeast Woodland", with coordinates that are correct by construction.
+
+Pass `--use-ai` to have an OpenAI vision model describe the areas instead (more evocative names, needs `OPENAI_API_KEY` and a matching `.png` beside the `.tmx`).
+
